ile_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='defned action of contraceptive?    [NEET-I, 2016]\n\n\n\n\n\ngenes that show 50 per cent recombination frequency\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n|a) Barrier methods|Prevent fertilization|\n|---|---|\n|b) Intra uterine<br>devices|Increases phagocytosis of sperms,<br>suppresses sperm motility and<br>fertilizing capacity of sperms|\n|c)Hormonal<br>contraceptives|Prevent retard entry of sperms,<br>prevent ovulation and fertilization|\n|** d) Vasectomy**|**Prevents spermatogenesis**|\n\n\n**Chapter 4 - Principle of Inheritance and Variation**\n\n\n\n1. \x07The fruit fly _Drosophila_ melanogaster was found\n\nto be very suitable for experimental verification of\nchromosomal theory of inheritance by Morgan and\nhis colleagues because      [AIPMT MAINS 2010]\na) \x07It reproduces parthenogenetically\nb) \x07A single mating produces two young flies\nc) Smaller female is easily recognizable from large male\n**d) \x07It completes the life cycle in about two weeks**\n2. \x07Which one of the following cannot be explained on\n\n\n\nthe basis of Mendel’s Law of Dominance?\n\n\n\n\n[AIPMT PRE 2010]\na) The discrete unit controlling a particular character'), Document(id='a511ce85-d9a6-4b98-84ec-4c11b34d7e6c', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='_�����������������������_\n_�����������������������_\n_�����������������������_\n_�����������������������_\n\n\n\n_�����������������������������_\n_�����������������������������_\n_�����������������������������_\n\n\n\n_�����������������������_\n_�����������������������_\n_�����������������������_\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n_����������������������_\n_����������������������_\n_����������������������_\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nMolecular Genetics **72**\n\n\nundergoes polymerisation under oxygen\ntension causing the change in the shape of\nthe RBC from biconcave to a sickle shaped\nstructure.\n\n\nThe effect of point mutation can be\nunderstood by the following example.\n\n**ABC DEF GHI JKL**\n\nIf we insert a letter **O** between **DEF**\nand **GHI** the arrangement would be\n\n**ABC DEF OGH IJK L**\n\nIf we insert **OQ** at the same place the\narrangement would be\n\n**ABC DEF OQG HIJ KL**\n\n\nThe above information shows that insertion\nor deletion of one or two bases, changes the\nreading frame from the point of insertions or\ndeletions. Such mutations are referred to as\nframe shift insertion or deletion mutations.\nThis forms the genetic basis of proof that codon\nis a triplet and is read in a continuous manner\n\n\n**5.11. tRNA – the adapter molecule**\n\nThe transfer RNA, (tRNA) molecule of a\ncell acts as a vehicle that picks up the amino\nacids scattered through the cytoplasm and\nalso reads specific codes of mRNA molecules.\nHence it is called an **adapter molecule.** This\nterm was postulated by Francis Crick.'), Document(id='fbe87405-71b8-4ac0-9cda-22ccc396c3fc', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='fusion\niii. \x07Meiosis never occurs during sexual reproduction\niv. \x07External fertilization is a rule during sexual\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nreproduction\nChoose the correct statements from the options\nbelow:\na) i and iv b) i and ii\nc) ii and iii **d) i and iv**\n4. Given below are a few statements related to external\nfertilization. Choose the correct statements:\ni. \x07The male and female gametes are formed and\n\n\n|option using the code|es given below [NEET 2016, phase I]|\n|---|---|\n|Column I|Column II|\n|A. Mons pubis|1.Embryo formation|\n|B. Antrum|2. Sperm|\n|C. Trophectoderm|3. Female external genitalia|\n|D. Nebenkem|4. Graafan follicle|\n\n\n\nreleased simultaneously\nii. \x07Only a few gametes are released into the medium\niii. \x07Water is the medium in a majority of organisms\n\nexhibiting external fertilization\niv. \x07Offspring formed as a result of external\n\n\n\nA B C D\na) 3 4 2 1\n**b)** **3** **4** **1** **2**\nc) 3 1 4 2\nd) 1 4 3 2\n5. \x07Which one of the following is not the function of\n\n\n\nplacenta? (NEET, 2013)\na) \x07To facilitate supply of oxygen and nutrients to embryo\nb) \x07To secrete oestrogen\nc) \x07To facilitate the removal of carbondioxide and'), Document(id='9631a5fd-5376-4a15-87e3-bc459756ab90', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='Molecular Genetics **74**\n\n\n\nvariable loop or extra arm. The amino acid\nis attached to one end (amino acid acceptor\nend) and the other end consists of three\nanticodon nucleotides. The anticodon pairs\nwith a codon in mRNA ensuring that the\ncorrect amino acid is incorporated into the\ngrowing polypeptide chain. Four different\nregions of double-stranded RNA are formed\nduring the folding process. Modified bases\nare especially common in tRNA. Wobbling\nbetween anticodon and codon allows some\ntRNA molecules to read more than one codon.\nThe process of addition of amino acid\nto tRNA is known as **aminoacylation** or\n**charging** and the resultant product is called\naminoacyl- tRNA (charged tRNA). Without\naminoacylation tRNA is known as uncharged\ntRNA **(Fig. 5.12).** If two such tRNAs are\nbrought together peptide bond formation is\nfavoured energetically. Numbers of amino\nacids are joined by peptide bonds to form a\npolypeptide chain. This aminoacylation is\ncatalyzed by an enzyme **aminoacyl – tRNA**\n**synthetase** . This is an endothermic reaction\nand is associated with ATP hydrolysis. 20\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n~~����~~\n\n\n\n~~�����~~\n\n\n|&<br>&<br>8<br>&<br>*<br>&|Col2|Col3|\n|---|---|---|\n||||\n||||\n||||\n||||\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n**Fig. 5.11 Holley’s two-dimensional clover**\n\n**leaf model of transfer RNA**\n\n\n\n\n15. Give reasons: Genetic code is ‘universal’.\n\n16. Name the parts marked ‘A’ and ‘B’ in the\n\ngiven transcription unit:'), Document(id='e7cf7b50-08f3-434d-a2c6-2032b3e5812c', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='The common types of interspecific\ninter actions are:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n|r selected species<br>(r - Reproductive<br>capacity)|K selected species<br>(K - Carrying<br>capacity)|\n|---|---|\n|Smaller sized<br>organisms|Larger sized<br>organisms|\n|Produce many<br>offspring|Produce few<br>offspring|\n|Mature early|Late maturity with<br>extended parental<br>care|\n|Short life<br>expectancy|Long life expectancy|\n|Each individual<br>reproduces only<br>once or few times in<br>their life time|Can reproduce<br>more than once in<br>lifetime|\n|Only few reach<br>adulthood|Most individuals<br>reach maximum life<br>span|\n|Unstable<br>environment,<br>density independent|Stable environment,<br>density dependent|\n\n\n\nOrganisms and Populations **176**\n\n\n**Table 10.3 Analysis of two species population interactions**'), Document(id='b651d2d0-ac90-4e61-88bf-998f27ac7a02', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='�������������\n������������\n���������������\n\n���������������\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n|Col1|Col2|\n|---|---|\n|RIIVSULQJV<br>$$<br><br>$D<br><br><br><br>AA<br>AA|RIIVSULQJV<br>$$<br><br>$D<br><br><br><br>AA<br>AA|\n|DD<br>DD<br>aa|DD<br>DD<br>aa|\n|$D<br>$D<br><br>DD<br>DD<br>DD<br><br>aa<br>aa<br><br>AA|$D<br>$D<br><br>DD<br>DD<br>DD<br><br>aa<br>aa<br><br>AA|\n|**101**<br>Evolution<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03$\x03 \x03\x13\x11\x16<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03D\x03 \x03\x13\x11\x1a<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03$\x03 \x03\x13\x11\x13<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03D\x03 \x03\x14\x11\x13<br><br><br>**Fig 6.9 Genetic drif**<br>aa<br>AA<br>**Fig 6.8 Gene fow**<br>**XII Std Biology-Zoology Chapter-6 EM.indd   101**<br>**23/12/2022   12:25:51**|**101**<br>Evolution<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03$\x03 \x03\x13\x11\x16<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03D\x03 \x03\x13\x11\x1a<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03$\x03 \x03\x13\x11\x13<br>)UHTXHQF\\\x03RI\x03D\x03 \x03\x14\x11\x13<br><br><br>**Fig 6.9 Genetic drif**<br>aa<br>AA<br>**Fig 6.8 Gene fow**<br>**XII Std Biology-Zoology Chapter-6 EM.indd   101**<br>**23/12/2022   12:25:51**|\n|||\n\n\n**Table 7.1. Bacterial diseases in human beings**'), Document(id='70526b72-9955-4373-8bee-65175dcccb4d', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='[AIPMT PRE 2010]\na) \x07Three b) \x07One **c) \x07Four** d) \x07Two\n4. \x07Which one of the following symbols and its\n\nrepresentation, used in human pedigree analysis is\ncorrect? [AIPMT PRE 2010]\n**a) \x07□=○=Mating between relatives**\nb) \x07○=Unaffected male\nc) \x07□=Unaffected female\nd) \x07◊=Male affected\n5. \x07Which one of the following conditions correctly\n\n\n\nstatements is incorrect? [NEET-I, 2016]\na) It is usually done when a woman is between 14-16\n\n\n\nfemale sex in birds\n6. \x07A normal-visioned man whose father was blind,\n\n\n\ndescribes the manner of determining the sex in the\ngiven example? [AIPMT PRE 2011]\n**a) \x07XO type of sex chromosomes determine male**\n\n**sex in grasshopper**\nb) XO condition in humans as found in Turner\n\nsyndrome, determines female sex\nc) Homozygous sex chromosomes(XX) produce\n\nmale in Drosophila\nd) Homozygous sex chromosomes(ZZ) determine\n\n\n\nmarries a woman  whose father was also colour\nblind. They have their first child as a daughter. What\nare the chances that this child would be colour blind?\n\n\n\nweeks pregnant\nb) It is used for prenatal sex determination\nc) It can be used for detection of Down syndrome\n**d) \x07It can be used for detection of Cleft palate**\n4. \x07Which of the following approach does not give the\n\n\n\n\n[AIPMT PRE 2012]\na) \x07100% **b) \x070%** c) \x0725% d) \x0750%\n7. \x07Which of the following statements is not true of two\n\n\n\ndefned action of contraceptive?    [NEET-I, 2016]\n\n\n\n\n\ngenes that show 50 per cent recombination frequency'), Document(id='a9494efc-2adb-4c21-bdc3-5f53c8d8df80', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content="**91** Evolution\n\n\n\n\n(c)\n\n\n\n(a)\n\n\n\n(b)\n\n\n\n**Fig 6.7 Operation of natural selection on different traits (a) Stabilising (b) Directional and (c) Disruptive**\n\n\n\nor more different species. It is also called\nadaptive radiation. E.g. Darwin's finchesbeak size in relation to seed size inhabiting\nGalapagos islands.\n\nGroup selection and sexual selection are\nother types of selection. The two major group\nselections are Altrusim and Kin selection.\n\n**6.6.2 Gene flow**\n\nMovement of genes through gametes or\nmovement of individuals in (immigration) and\nout (emigration) of a population is referred to as\ngene flow. Organisms and gametes that enter the\npopulation may have new alleles or may bring\nin existing alleles but in different proportions\nthan those already in the population. Gene flow\ncan be a strong agent of evolution **(Fig 6.8).**\n\n\nAA\n\n\n\n**6.6.3 Genetic drift / Sewall**\n**Wright Effect**\n\nGenetic drift is a mechanism of evolution in\nwhich allele frequencies of a population change\nover generation due to chance (sampling error).\nGenetic drift occurs in all population sizes,\nbut its effects are strong in a small population\n**(Fig. 6.9).** It may result in a loss of some\nalleles (including beneficial ones) and fixation\nof other alleles. Genetic drift can have major\neffects, when the population is reduced in size\nby natural disaster due to bottle neck effect or\nwhen a small group of population splits from\nthe main population to form a new colony due\nto founder’s effect."), Document(id='039c8e62-ca49-4247-af83-44c85379d10a', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='**(a) Inhibition of spermatogenesis.**\n\n(b) Inhibition of ovulation.\n\n(c) \x07Changes in cervical mucus impairing\n\nits ability to allow passage and\ntransport of sperms.\n\n(d) \x07Alteration in uterine endometrium to\n\nmake it unsuitable for implantation.\n\n\n9. Select the correct term from the bracket\n\nand complete the given branching tree\n\n\n\n**Periodic abstinence**\n\n\n\n10. Correct the following statements\n\na) \x07Transfer of an ovum collected from\n\ndonor into the fallopian tube is called\nZIFT.\n\nb) \x07Transfering of an embryo with more\n\nthan 8 blastomeres into uterus is\ncalled GIFT.\n\nc) \x07Multiload 375 is a hormone releasing\n\nIUD.\n\n11. Which method do you suggest the couple\n\nto have a baby, if the male partner fails to\ninseminate the female or due to very low\nsperm count in the ejaculate?\n\n12. Expand the following\n\na) ZIFT b) ICSI\n\n13. What are the strategies to be\n\nimplemented in India to attain total\nreproductive health?\n\n14. Differentiate foeticide and infanticide.\n\n15. Describe the major STDs and their\n\nsymptoms.\n\n16. How are STDs transmitted?\n\n17. Write the preventive measures of STDs.\n\n18. The procedure of GIFT involves the\n\ntransfer of female gametes into the\nfallopain tube, can gametes be transferred\nto the uterus to achieve the same result?\nExplain.\n\n19. Amniocentesis, the foetal sex\ndetermination test, is banned in our\ncountry, Is it necessary? Comment.\n\n20. Explain the various barrier methods to\n\ncontrol human population.\n\n21. Open Book Assessment'), Document(id='49c22deb-4ff4-47ba-a5df-f836bdc9c911', metadata={'source': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'user_id': 'test', 'file_name': 'Class_12_Bio_Zoology_English_2024_Edition-www.tntextbooks.in.pdf', 'document_id': '2f6820b6-9f4f-4fee-94fa-419f3d35bac6'}, page_content='**a) Extinction** b) Mutation\n\nc) Amensalism d) Symbiosis\n\n\n6. Which of the following is an r-species\n\na) Human **b) Insects**\n\nc) Rhinoceros d) Whale\n\n\n7. Match the following and choose the correct\n\ncombination from the options given below.\n\nColumn I Column II\n\nA. Mutalism 1. Lion and deer\n\nB. Commensalism 2. \x07Round worm and man\n\nC. Parasitism 3. \x07Birds compete with\nsquirrels for nuts\n\nD. Competition 4. \x07Sea anemone on hermit\n\ncrab\n\nE. Predation 5. \x07Barnacles attached to\n\nWhales\n\n\nOrganisms and Populations **178**\n\n\n\na) **A- 4, B-5, C-2, D –3, E-1**\nb) A- 3, B-1, C-4, D – 2, E-5\nc) A- 2, B-3, C-1, D – 5, E-4\nd) A- 5, B-4, C-2, D – 3, E-1\n\n8. The figure given below is a diagrammatic\n\nrepresentation of response of organisms\nto abiotic factors. What do A, 